        'en' => [
            'title' => 'Low-sodium salt in kidney disease: the front label is not the whole story',
            'image_alt' => 'Two neutral salt jars on a light background. A magnifying glass hovers over the right jar, its lens showing teal salt crystals. Purple and teal accents, clean illustration without text.',
            'excerpt' => 'The front of the pack says less sodium, but the ingredient list adds potassium chloride. When kidney function is impaired, potassium can build up in the blood and disturb the heart rhythm. Read the whole label and talk to your doctor before making the swap.',
            'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>On the shelf next to regular salt you will now find a box labelled “low-sodium salt”. Turn it over and the ingredient list tells you that the manufacturer has replaced part of the sodium chloride with potassium chloride, KCl. With the same amount of such a blend you take in less sodium and more potassium. The taste stays similar, so this salt looks like the obvious choice, especially when you are trying to salt less.</p>
<p>A healthy kidney handles this difference. It excretes potassium in the urine, so potassium does not build up in the blood. In chronic kidney disease, especially in advanced stages, the situation is different: potassium can accumulate, and a markedly elevated level can disturb the heart rhythm. The risk is increased by some common medicines, for example spironolactone.</p>
<p>In its 2025 guideline, the World Health Organization therefore conditionally recommends potassium-based salt substitutes for adults in the general population who use salt in cooking or at the table. It explicitly excludes people with impaired kidney function, as well as children, pregnant women and people taking potassium-sparing medicines. (<a href="https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240105591">WHO, Use of lower-sodium salt substitutes, 2025</a>)</p>
<p>The evidence behind this comes from the large randomised SSaSS trial in China. Nearly 21,000 people from 600 villages used a salt made of about 75% sodium chloride and 25% potassium chloride for five years. In this group there were fewer strokes, cardiovascular events and deaths than in the group using regular salt. However, people with known serious kidney disease, users of potassium supplements and potassium-sparing medicines were excluded, and nobody monitored blood potassium levels regularly throughout the study. (<a href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2106754">Neal et al., SSaSS, New England Journal of Medicine 2021</a>) Reliable data on the safety of potassium-based salt substitutes in chronic kidney disease are therefore still lacking. The joint ESC and ERA guideline on cardiovascular disease in chronic kidney disease from 2026 says the same in so many words: better data on the safety of potassium-based salt substitutes in CKD are still needed, precisely because of the risk of elevated blood potassium. (<a href="https://www.escardio.org/guidelines/clinical-practice-guidelines/all-esc-practice-guidelines/cvd-chronic-kidney-disease">ESC/ERA 2026</a>)</p>
<p>What does this mean when you shop? You can start salting less simply: add less salt and compare the salt content per 100 g between similar products. The KDIGO recommendations for chronic kidney disease advise a sodium intake below 2 grams per day, which corresponds to less than 5 grams of table salt. (<a href="https://kdigo.org/guidelines/ckd-evaluation-and-management/">KDIGO 2024, chapter 3</a>)</p>
<p>If you have chronic kidney disease and the label lists potassium chloride, photograph the ingredient list and, before using the product regularly, talk to your doctor or to a dietitian who specialises in kidney disease. Whether a particular salt substitute suits you also depends on your blood test results and the medicines you take.</p>
<p>The front of the box talks about sodium. The kidney reading the back of the box sees potassium too. So before you swap your salt, always read the whole ingredient list and discuss it with the person who knows your blood results.</p>
<p><em>This article is for information only and does not replace individual medical advice. Whether a specific product is suitable for you is decided by your treating physician based on your test results and the medicines you take.</em></p>

